<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<CustomField xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
        THIS COMMENT SITS INSIDE THE ROOT ELEMENT DELIBERATELY. A comment placed ABOVE
        <CustomField> breaks `sf` deploy at source conversion with a misleading
        "unable to find matching parent xml file" error.

        =====================================================================================
        WHAT THIS FIELD IS. The matched broker's firm (Account), derived from Broker__c at the
        same email-match intake step. See Broker__c.field-meta.xml in this same folder for the
        full USER DECISION writeup (match-only intake, blank is the normal state on most Leads,
        conversion-time Contact/Account creation lives in LeadConvertMatchService).
        =====================================================================================
        This field is populated ONLY alongside Broker__c, by the same match; it is never
        stamped independently of a resolved broker Contact. When Broker__c is blank, this field
        is blank too, for the identical reason (no sender-email match found).

        =====================================================================================
        NAMING — ARCHITECTURE.md Section 1 relationship-field rule. CONFIRMED, WITH A DOCUMENTED
        INTERPRETATION CALL — read this in full before assuming the rule was applied loosely.
        =====================================================================================
        The rule's literal text: "The field name is the target object's name. Exception:
        role-named lookups to User / Contact take the role name — API names must be unique, so a
        second User__c cannot exist." Read narrowly, that exception clause names only User and
        Contact — this field references Account, which is not listed.

        Read for its OWN stated reasoning rather than its example list, the exception exists
        because a plain object-name field (e.g. "Account__c") cannot express a ROLE when one is
        needed, and because API-name uniqueness means only one field on an object can ever be
        literally named after the target object. Both of those reasons apply here exactly as
        written, independent of which object is being referenced:
          1. "Account__c" on Lead would be actively ambiguous — Lead already carries a distinct
             concept of its own company (`Company`, standard Text), and Salesforce's own
             standard Lead-to-Account CONVERSION already overloads "the Account" to mean "the
             Account this Lead becomes." A field literally named Account__c would collide with
             that conversion semantic on the very object where conversion happens. Broker_Firm__c
             cannot be confused with either.
          2. There is exactly ONE existing Account lookup in this repo today —
             Receivables_Summary__c.Account__c — and it correctly uses the plain object name
             because it IS the generic, non-role relationship the base rule describes ("the
             Account this receivable summary is for," nothing more specific). It is not a
             counter-precedent against role-naming; it is confirmation the base rule is still
             correct for a GENERIC Account reference, and this is not one.

        CONCLUSION: Broker_Firm__c is the correct name under the rule's own reasoning, but this
        is the FIRST role-named Account lookup in the org, and ARCHITECTURE.md Section 1's
        exception clause does not literally list Account. Recording this here rather than
        silently extending the rule: a future ARCHITECTURE.md edit should probably widen the
        exception's wording from "User / Contact" to "User / Contact / Account" (or drop the
        object list entirely in favor of the reasoning above) so the next reader does not have to
        re-derive this. Not changed here — ARCHITECTURE.md is out of scope for this task and is
        owned elsewhere.

        This field's exact API name was set by the concurrent developer agent building the
        matching Apex against it; had the analysis above concluded a different name was required,
        this file would not have been created and the conflict would have been raised instead.

        COLLISION CHECK, MEASURED NOT ASSUMED. The only existing referenceTo=Account field in
        this repo is Receivables_Summary__c.Account__c -> relationshipName Receivables_Summaries.
        "Brokered_Leads" is unused on Account. (It is reused as the relationshipName on the
        SIBLING Contact lookup Lead.Broker__c in this same change — that is not a collision:
        relationshipName is scoped per PARENT object, Contact and Account are different parents,
        and "Brokered Leads" reads correctly as a related-list label from either firm's or
        either broker's Account/Contact page.)

        =====================================================================================
        NO lookupFilter ON THIS FIELD, DELIBERATELY — same reasoning as Broker__c.
        =====================================================================================
        This is a machine-derived field, not a human pick from a Lookup widget. An ACTIVE
        non-optional lookupFilter (e.g. restricting to Account.RecordType.DeveloperName =
        'Broker_Firm') is enforced on Apex DML in this org, not merely the UI — see
        Broker__c.field-meta.xml's note on Opportunity.Broker__c. Filtering here would risk
        turning a routine intake write into a hard failure whenever the matched broker
        Contact's own Account did not carry the Broker_Firm record type for any data-quality
        reason. No filter added.

        =====================================================================================
        deleteConstraint = SetNull, not Restrict, not Cascade — identical reasoning to Broker__c.
        =====================================================================================
        Advisory link to a firm Account that may later be deleted, merged, or reparented.
        Restrict would block legitimate Account cleanup for as long as any Lead referenced the
        firm. Cascade would delete Leads whenever a broker's firm Account was removed — active
        data loss on the wrong object. SetNull is the only choice consistent with Broker__c and
        with every Contact-lookup sibling cited there.

        =====================================================================================
        FLS — DECLARED HERE, NOT DEPLOYED HERE. This agent does not touch permission sets.
        =====================================================================================
        Same measured risk as Broker__c: an ungranted field on Lead throws System.DmlException
        and fails the whole intake transaction, not merely hides the field.

        Needed: DPEG_Acquisition_Edit — Read + EDIT on Lead.Broker_Firm__c, matching the
        Read+Edit grant already given to Parse_Confidence__c / Deal_Notes__c / First_Seen_Date__c
        on that same permission set, and matching the grant this change also asks for on the
        sibling Broker__c field.

        Measured: Broker_Protection_Access and DPEG_Admin_Access currently carry ZERO
        FieldPermissions rows for those three sibling fields. Same recommendation as Broker__c:
        add Read+Edit on Broker_Protection_Access (this is broker-identity data squarely inside
        that set's domain); leave DPEG_Admin_Access to the P8 reviewer's judgment. Not decided
        here.
    -->
    <fullName>Broker_Firm__c</fullName>
    <label>Broker Firm</label>
    <type>Lookup</type>
    <referenceTo>Account</referenceTo>
    <relationshipLabel>Brokered Leads</relationshipLabel>
    <relationshipName>Brokered_Leads</relationshipName>
    <required>false</required>
    <deleteConstraint>SetNull</deleteConstraint>
    <description>The matched broker's firm, derived from Broker__c at the same email-match intake step. Blank whenever Broker__c is blank — no sender-email match found at intake, which is the expected state on most Leads. Never stamped independently of Broker__c. Contact/Account creation for a non-match happens later, at conversion, in LeadConvertMatchService.</description>
    <inlineHelpText>Filled in automatically alongside Broker, from that broker Contact's Account. Stays blank whenever Broker is blank.</inlineHelpText>
    <trackHistory>false</trackHistory>
    <trackTrending>false</trackTrending>
</CustomField>
